{
  "language": "en",
  "sections": {
    "1": {
      "status": "ready",
      "title": "Poem 1",
      "stanzas": [
        [
          "All was well—",
          "cows grazed",
          "across the field,",
          "on the pasture’s carpet."
        ],
        [
          "Grass flowers bloomed",
          "in every colour.",
          "A cowherd boy",
          "whistled",
          "from a kadamba branch;",
          "snakes and other reptiles",
          "listened to the tune.",
          "A quarrel broke out.",
          "Neither women nor gold were at stake,",
          "yet the quarrel grew."
        ],
        [
          "Worry took hold:",
          "was some calamity",
          "about to strike?",
          "Would the villages, fields and woods burn?",
          "Would the wind strip people of their clothes?",
          "Would the flute notes",
          "drifting from the kadamba branch die away?",
          "Would the sky and stars",
          "fall in pieces?",
          "Would the fierce sun’s radiance and pride",
          "blow away",
          "as a handful of ash?",
          "Would every thread",
          "binding heart to heart",
          "snap?",
          "Would anarchy, that python,",
          "swallow the country",
          "and its government?"
        ],
        [
          "Then, dispelling",
          "those fears filling the sky,",
          "from who knows where",
          "a sturdy young fellow came running,",
          "like a golden morning.",
          "From a high mound of earth",
          "he gave orders with a king’s bearing.",
          "His companions sat below",
          "like subjects,",
          "and the uproar died",
          "in a moment."
        ],
        [
          "There was no magic",
          "in the Vikramadityas.",
          "As the earth was dug away, it became clear—",
          "beneath that high mound",
          "a throne lay hidden."
        ]
      ]
    },
    "21": {
      "status": "ready",
      "title": "Poem 21",
      "stanzas": [
        [
          "The gods, too,",
          "knew a thing or two!",
          "From time to time,",
          "without a word to anyone,",
          "they would mount a black horse, a white horse,",
          "and set out",
          "to conquer the world."
        ],
        [
          "The story",
          "grew thrilling",
          "when, slipping a jewelled ring",
          "onto the milk-seller’s hand,",
          "the Lords took every bit from the milk pot—",
          "the milk, even what it left behind—",
          "all in secret.",
          "Word of the Maharaja’s might",
          "travelled a thousand kos",
          "from mouth to mouth:",
          "the gods, in the king’s service!"
        ],
        [
          "Had no such story",
          "been fashioned,",
          "tell me—",
          "who would bring offerings,",
          "and why,",
          "to this half-carved wooden doll,",
          "this uncomprehending image,",
          "in the Great Temple,",
          "on the jewelled altar,",
          "with an innocent child’s",
          "simple faith?"
        ]
      ]
    },
    "24": {
      "status": "ready",
      "title": "Poem 24",
      "stanzas": [
        [
          "Who is a subject, who a king?",
          "Who is Kapila, who Kapileshwar?"
        ],
        [
          "When water pours from the pot",
          "in an elephant’s trunk,",
          "fortune changes.",
          "A cowherd boy, as in a dream,",
          "becomes",
          "Gajapati, Pashupatipati,",
          "mightiest of heroes,",
          "king of kings…"
        ],
        [
          "Once he is seated",
          "on the throne,",
          "there is no getting",
          "to see Kapila.",
          "A knot of idlers watches",
          "the Kasias turn back",
          "from the Lion Gate,",
          "while the moustached kotwal",
          "and armed gatekeepers",
          "bar the way",
          "with their strange airs."
        ]
      ]
    }
  }
}
